\title{Scheduling a Finite Portfolio over an Invisible Channel:\\
An Exact Value-of-Information Theory}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact, finite and entirely elementary theory of algorithm-portfolio
scheduling under partial observation, motivated by a measured phenomenon in
integer factorisation: a five-member factoring portfolio in which no member is a
universal winner, in which winner shares are flat across every feature computable
from the input, and in which a tuned routing rule improves the expected cost by
exactly zero while a learned routing rule is significantly \emph{worse} than the
best fixed member.

The central object is the \emph{dial value} $D(\varphi)$ of an observation map
$\varphi$: the expected cost of the best rule measurable with respect to
$\varphi$. We prove that every rule decomposes over the fibers of $\varphi$, that
$D(\varphi)=\sum_o \min_s V(o,s)$ where $V$ is the unnormalised conditional cost
table, and that this value is attained. From this we obtain the information
ladder $\E[\Or]\le D(\varphi)\le B$, where $B$ is the best static value;
monotonicity of $D$ under refinement; and an exact characterisation:
$D(\varphi)<B$ if and only if every member of the portfolio is strictly beaten on
some fiber.

We introduce \emph{invisibility} of an observation (conditional means constant
across fibers) and prove that it forces $D(\varphi)=B$, with a strict converse:
any rule deviating toward a suboptimal member on a positive-mass fiber is
strictly worse than doing nothing. We then make the hypothesis quantitative: an
$\varepsilon$-invisible observation satisfies $B-D(\varphi)\le 2\varepsilon$, and
the constant $2$ is optimal, witnessed by an explicit anti-diagonal family with
gap $2n/(n+1)$; the naive converse fails. Refining this, the dial gain equals the
smallest \emph{fiberwise regret} in the portfolio, so a null measurement
certifies exactly the existence of a fiberwise champion; for a two-member
portfolio the gain is exactly the minimum of two \emph{swap masses}; and this
pairwise certificate is invisible at portfolio level even on irredundant
portfolios. On the statistics of regret we prove median blindness, a reverse
Markov bound that converts a measured mean and a cost cap into a forced tail
mass, and an exact finite layer-cake identity showing that stochastic dominance
implies a mean inequality while the converse --- and hence mean-based elimination
--- fails. Finally we identify the hidden channel as the $B$-powersmoothness of
$p-1$, exhibit two $21$-bit balanced semiprimes with identical visible profile
and opposite smoothness class, prove that a budget-capped $p-1$ probe detects the
smooth class, and show by a discrete Topkis theorem that an ordered probe with
decreasing differences admits an optimal threshold schedule.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} algorithm portfolios, value of information, regret,
invisible channel, powersmoothness, stochastic dominance, Topkis monotonicity,
reverse Markov inequality.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical cell}

Consider a portfolio of five classical integer-factorisation methods run on
semiprimes $N=pq$: Pollard's rho, the $p-1$ method with bound $256$, the $p-1$
method with bound $1024$, Fermat's difference-of-squares method, and trial
division. Over a broad sample, the \emph{oracle winner shares} --- the fraction of
instances on which each method finishes first --- are approximately
\[
\rho:\ 0.580,\quad (p-1)@256:\ 0.345,\quad (p-1)@1024:\ 0.045,\quad
\text{Fermat}:\ 0.028,\quad \text{trial division}:\ 0.002 .
\]

Three facts about this table are the subject of this paper.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{No universal winner.} Every member wins on a set of positive
probability and loses on a set of positive probability.
\item \textbf{Flatness.} The shares are essentially constant across bit-length
quintiles and across balance quintiles --- indeed across every measured statistic
computable from $N$ alone.
\item \textbf{Null scheduling.} A tuned routing rule built from those visible
statistics improves the expected cost by $\Delta=0.000$; the best static member
has expected regret $3.117$ against the oracle; and a learned routing rule
achieves $4.683$, i.e.\ it is significantly worse than doing nothing. The median
regret ratio is exactly $1.000$ for every strategy considered.
\end{enumerate}

The explanation is that the organising variable is not visible: for the $p-1$
family the winner is decided by the \emph{powersmoothness of $p-1$}, a property of
the factorisation of $N$ and hence of the very thing being computed. This paper
turns that explanation into theorems, and in doing so obtains a complete
value-of-information theory for finite portfolios.

All statements are finite and rational; no measure theory is used.

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Portfolio problem]
A \emph{finite portfolio problem} consists of a finite instance space $\Omega$
with weights $w:\Omega\to\Q$, $w\ge 0$, $\sum_\omega w(\omega)=1$; a finite
nonempty set $S$ of \emph{members}; and a cost $c:\Omega\times S\to\Q$. We write
$\E[f]=\sum_\omega w(\omega)f(\omega)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Benchmarks]
The \emph{oracle cost} is $\Or(\omega)=\min_{s\in S}c(\omega,s)$; the
\emph{best static value} is $B=\min_{s}\E[c(\cdot,s)]$; the \emph{static regret}
is $R=B-\E[\Or]$.
\end{definition}

Since $\Or\le c(\cdot,s)$ pointwise and $w\ge0$, we have $R\ge 0$.

\begin{definition}[Observation, rule, fiber]
An \emph{observation} is a map $\varphi:\Omega\to O$ into a finite set $O$. A
\emph{rule} (or \emph{dial}) is a map $\pi:O\to S$, costing
$c(\omega,\pi(\varphi(\omega)))$ on $\omega$. For $o\in O$ put
\[
\mu(o)=\!\!\sum_{\omega:\varphi(\omega)=o}\!\! w(\omega),
\qquad
V(o,s)=\!\!\sum_{\omega:\varphi(\omega)=o}\!\! w(\omega)\,c(\omega,s),
\]
the \emph{fiber mass} and the \emph{fiber value}. Then $\mu\ge0$ and
$\sum_o\mu(o)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dial value]
$D(\varphi)=\sum_{o\in O}\min_{s\in S}V(o,s)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Fiberwise decomposition and the information ladder}

\begin{theorem}[Fiberwise decomposition]\label{thm:fiber}
For every rule $\pi:O\to S$,
$\E[c(\cdot,\pi(\varphi(\cdot)))]=\sum_{o}V(o,\pi(o))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition $\Omega$ into the fibers of $\varphi$. On the fiber over $o$ we have
$\varphi(\omega)=o$, so $c(\omega,\pi(\varphi(\omega)))=c(\omega,\pi(o))$ and the
inner sum is $V(o,\pi(o))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:const}
$\E[c(\cdot,s)]=\sum_o V(o,s)$ for every member $s$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The dial value is the optimum, and is attained]\label{thm:dialopt}
For every rule $\pi$ we have $D(\varphi)\le \E[c(\cdot,\pi(\varphi(\cdot)))]$,
and some rule $\pi^\star$ attains $D(\varphi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:fiber} the cost is $\sum_o V(o,\pi(o))\ge\sum_o\min_s V(o,s)$.
For attainment choose $\pi^\star(o)\in\arg\min_s V(o,s)$, possible since $S$ is
finite and nonempty.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[An optimised dial never hurts]\label{thm:dialleB}
$D(\varphi)\le B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $s_0$ attain $B$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:const},
$B=\sum_o V(o,s_0)\ge\sum_o\min_sV(o,s)=D(\varphi)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The oracle bounds the dial from below]\label{thm:oracledial}
$\E[\Or]\le D(\varphi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For an optimal rule $\pi^\star$ we have $\Or(\omega)\le
c(\omega,\pi^\star(\varphi(\omega)))$ pointwise; take expectations, using
$w\ge0$, and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dialopt}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Information ladder]
$\E[\Or]\le D(\varphi)\le B$.
\end{corollary}

The two gaps are the \emph{dial gain} $B-D(\varphi)$ and the static regret
$R=B-\E[\Or]$.

\begin{theorem}[Monotone value of information]\label{thm:mono}
Let $\varphi:\Omega\to O$ and $\psi:\Omega\to O'$ satisfy $\varphi=g\circ\psi$ for
some $g:O'\to O$ (so $\psi$ refines $\varphi$). Then $D(\psi)\le D(\varphi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\pi^\star$ be optimal for $\varphi$. The $\psi$-measurable rule
$\pi^\star\circ g$ has the same pointwise cost as $\pi^\star$, since
$\pi^\star(g(\psi(\omega)))=\pi^\star(\varphi(\omega))$. Conclude by
Theorem~\ref{thm:dialopt}.
\end{proof}

In particular the finest observation $\varphi=\mathrm{id}$ gives
$D(\mathrm{id})=\E[\Or]$.

\begin{theorem}[Dial-edge criterion]\label{thm:dialedge}
$D(\varphi)<B$ if and only if for every member $s\in S$ there is a fiber $o$ with
$\min_{t}V(o,t)<V(o,s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Fix $s$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:const} and $B\le\E[c(\cdot,s)]$,
$\sum_o\min_tV(o,t)=D(\varphi)<B\le\sum_oV(o,s)$. Were $s$ fiberwise optimal
everywhere the two sums would agree; so $s$ is strictly beaten somewhere.
($\Leftarrow$) Let $s_0$ attain $B$ and let $o_0$ be a fiber where $s_0$ is
strictly beaten. Then $\min_tV(o,t)\le V(o,s_0)$ for all $o$, strictly at $o_0$;
summing gives $D(\varphi)<\sum_oV(o,s_0)=B$.
\end{proof}

A dial pays off \emph{only} when the portfolio has no fiberwise champion: a
single member minimising the conditional cost on every fiber renders every
observation-measurable rule worthless.

\section{Invisibility and the no-dial-edge theorem}

\begin{definition}[Invisibility]
$\varphi$ is \emph{invisible} with mean profile $m:S\to\Q$ if
$V(o,s)=\mu(o)m(s)$ for all $o\in O$, $s\in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:invmean}
Under invisibility $\E[c(\cdot,s)]=m(s)$ for every $s$, hence $B=\min_s m(s)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\E[c(\cdot,s)]=\sum_oV(o,s)=\bigl(\sum_o\mu(o)\bigr)m(s)=m(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No dial edge]\label{thm:nodial}
If $\varphi$ is invisible with profile $m$, then every rule $\pi$ satisfies
$B\le\E[c(\cdot,\pi(\varphi(\cdot)))]$: the optimal dial is the do-nothing dial.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:fiber} and invisibility the rule costs
$\sum_o\mu(o)m(\pi(o))$, a convex combination of values of $m$ (the masses are
nonnegative and sum to $1$), hence at least $\min_s m(s)=B$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:invmean}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A tuned rule can only hurt]\label{thm:strictworse}
If $\varphi$ is invisible with profile $m$, and $\pi$ deviates on a fiber $o_0$
with $\mu(o_0)>0$ toward a member with $m(\pi(o_0))>\min_s m(s)$, then
$B<\E[c(\cdot,\pi(\varphi(\cdot)))]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Compare $\sum_o\mu(o)m(\pi(o))$ termwise with $\sum_o\mu(o)\min_sm(s)=B$: every
term is $\ge$, and the term at $o_0$ is strictly larger since $\mu(o_0)>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Under invisibility $D(\varphi)=B$: the dial gain is exactly $0$.
\end{corollary}

Theorems~\ref{thm:nodial} and \ref{thm:strictworse} are the formal shadow of the
two measured facts: a tuned dial with $\Delta=0.000$, and a learned rule worse
than static. Consistently with Theorem~\ref{thm:dialedge}, under invisibility a
member minimising $m$ is a fiberwise champion.

\begin{theorem}[Paid probes]\label{thm:probe}
A probe revealing the instance exactly, at price $\kappa$ per instance, beats the
best static schedule if and only if $\kappa<R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A rule measurable for the identity observation attains $\E[\Or]$, so the total
cost is $\E[\Or]+\kappa$, and $\E[\Or]+\kappa<B \iff \kappa<B-\E[\Or]=R$.
\end{proof}

\section{Quantitative invisibility and stability}

\begin{definition}[$\varepsilon$-invisibility]
$\varphi$ is \emph{$\varepsilon$-invisible} with profile $m$ if
$\lvert V(o,s)-\mu(o)m(s)\rvert\le\varepsilon\,\mu(o)$ for all $o,s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:epsB}
If $\varphi$ is $\varepsilon$-invisible with profile $m$ then
$B\le\min_s m(s)+\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $s_0$ minimise $m$. Summing the upper bound over $o$ gives
$\E[c(\cdot,s_0)]\le\sum_o\mu(o)(m(s_0)+\varepsilon)=m(s_0)+\varepsilon$, and
$B\le\E[c(\cdot,s_0)]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:epsD}
If $\varphi$ is $\varepsilon$-invisible with profile $m$ then
$D(\varphi)\ge\min_s m(s)-\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On each fiber and for each $s$, $V(o,s)\ge\mu(o)(m(s)-\varepsilon)\ge
\mu(o)(\min_t m(t)-\varepsilon)$ using $\mu(o)\ge0$; minimise over $s$ and sum,
using $\sum_o\mu(o)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Stability of the no-dial-edge theorem]\label{thm:stability}
If $\varphi$ is $\varepsilon$-invisible then $B-D(\varphi)\le 2\varepsilon$, and
every rule $\pi$ satisfies $\E[c(\cdot,\pi(\varphi(\cdot)))]\ge B-2\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Subtract Theorem~\ref{thm:epsD} from Theorem~\ref{thm:epsB}; the second claim
follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:dialopt}.
\end{proof}

If moreover the dial is perfect, $D(\varphi)=\E[\Or]$, then $R\le 2\varepsilon$:
a nearly invisible observation cannot recover a large static regret.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the constant $2$]\label{thm:sharp}
For $n\ge1$ let $\Omega=S=O=\{0,\dots,n\}$ with uniform weights $1/(n+1)$, the
finest observation, and the anti-diagonal cost $c(\omega,s)=-1$ if $\omega=s$ and
$+1$ otherwise. This portfolio is $1$-invisible with profile $m\equiv0$, has
$B=(n-1)/(n+1)$, $D=-1$, and
\[
B-D=\frac{2n}{n+1}.
\]
Hence for every $\delta>0$ there is a $1$-invisible portfolio with dial gain
$>2-\delta$: the constant $2$ cannot be lowered.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each fiber is a singleton, so $\mu(o)=1/(n+1)$ and $V(o,s)=c(o,s)/(n+1)$,
whence $\lvert V(o,s)-0\rvert=\mu(o)$: exactly $1$-invisible. Each member costs
$-1$ once and $+1$ on the other $n$ instances, so $\E[c(\cdot,s)]=(n-1)/(n+1)$
and $B=(n-1)/(n+1)$; each singleton fiber contributes $-1/(n+1)$ to $D$, so
$D=-1$. Choose $n>2/\delta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The naive converse fails]\label{thm:noconverse}
Let $\Omega=\{0,1\}$ with $w\equiv 1/2$ and let every member cost $0$ on instance
$0$ and $2$ on instance $1$. Then the dial gain is $0$ on every observation, yet
the portfolio is not $\varepsilon$-invisible for any $\varepsilon<1$ and any
profile $m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All members have identical costs, so every rule costs what every constant rule
costs. With $\varphi=\mathrm{id}$: $\mu(0)=\mu(1)=1/2$, $V(0,s)=0$, $V(1,s)=1$;
the two conditions $\lvert 0-m(s)/2\rvert\le\varepsilon/2$ and
$\lvert 1-m(s)/2\rvert\le\varepsilon/2$ add to give $1\le\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\section{What a null measurement certifies}

\begin{definition}[Fiberwise regret]
$\FR(s)=\sum_{o}\bigl(V(o,s)-\min_tV(o,t)\bigr)\ge 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:FR}
$\FR(s)=\E[c(\cdot,s)]-D(\varphi)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The dial gain is an optimisation over members]\label{thm:gapmin}
$B-D(\varphi)=\min_{s\in S}\FR(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:FR} on minimising over $s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Null gain $=$ fiberwise champion]\label{thm:nullgain}
$D(\varphi)=B$ if and only if there is $s\in S$ with $V(o,s)=\min_tV(o,t)$ for
every fiber $o$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:gapmin}, $D(\varphi)=B$ iff $\FR(s)=0$ for some $s$; since
all summands of $\FR(s)$ are nonnegative, this happens iff $s$ attains every
fiberwise minimum.
\end{proof}

Thus the measured $\Delta=0.000$ certifies a fiberwise champion --- and, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:noconverse}, nothing about invisibility of the observation.

\begin{definition}[Swap mass]
For $f,g:O\to\Q$ put $\Sw(f,g)=\sum_o\max(f(o)-g(o),0)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two-member gap]\label{thm:twomember}
For $S=\{0,1\}$, writing $f(o)=V(o,0)$ and $g(o)=V(o,1)$,
\[
B-D(\varphi)=\min\bigl(\Sw(f,g),\Sw(g,f)\bigr),
\]
so a dial strictly helps precisely when both swap masses are positive.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$B=\min(\sum_of,\sum_og)$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:const} and
$D=\sum_o\min(f(o),g(o))$. Termwise, $\min(a,b)=a-(a-b)^+=b-(b-a)^+$, giving
$\sum_of-D=\Sw(f,g)$ and $\sum_og-D=\Sw(g,f)$; take the minimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A swap hidden by a third member]\label{thm:hidden}
There is an explicit three-member portfolio whose dial gain is exactly $0$ while
two of its members have swap masses $1$ in both directions.
\end{theorem}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:nullgain} the gain vanishes as soon as a third member is
fiberwise optimal everywhere, which constrains the other two not at all.

\begin{definition}[Irredundance]
The portfolio is \emph{irredundant} for $\varphi$ if for all $s\ne t$ there is a
fiber $o$ with $V(o,t)<V(o,s)$: no member is weakly beaten by another on every
fiber.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise structure is unbounded on irredundant portfolios]
\label{thm:irred}
For $0<e\le1$ let $\Omega=O=S=\{0,1,2\}$ with uniform weights $1/3$, the finest
observation, and cost matrix (rows $=$ instances, columns $=$ members)
\[
c=\begin{pmatrix}0&10&e\\ 10&0&e\\ 10&10&0\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Then the portfolio is irredundant, $D=0$, $B=2e/3$, so the dial gain is exactly
$2e/3$, while the swap masses of members $0$ and $1$ are $10/3$ in both
directions. Hence for every $M$ there is an irredundant three-member portfolio
with $M\cdot(B-D)<\min(\Sw,\Sw')$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fibers are singletons of mass $1/3$, so $V=c/3$. The row minima are all $0$, so
$D=0$; the column means are $20/3,20/3,2e/3$ and $e\le1$, so $B=2e/3$.
Irredundance: for $(s,t)=(0,1)$ take $o=1$; for $(1,0)$ take $o=0$; for $(s,2)$
with $s\le1$ take $o=2$; for $(2,s)$ take $o=s$, where $c(s,s)=0<e$ --- this uses
$e>0$. The swap masses are $10/3$ by direct computation. Finally choose $e$ with
$2Me/3<10/3$.
\end{proof}

\section{The statistics of regret, and elimination discipline}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{regret ratio} of member $s$ on $\omega$ is $c(\omega,s)/\Or(\omega)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Median blindness]\label{thm:median}
For every $M\ge0$ there is a two-instance, two-member portfolio with positive
oracle cost whose optimal static member ties the oracle on mass at least $1/2$
--- median regret ratio exactly $1$ --- while its mean regret ratio exceeds $M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\Omega=\{0,1\}$, $w=(3/4,1/4)$ and
$c=\begin{pmatrix}1&8M+8\\ 4M+4&1\end{pmatrix}$. The oracle costs $1$ on both
instances. The means are $\E[c(\cdot,0)]=M+7/4$ and $\E[c(\cdot,1)]=6M+25/4$, so
member $0$ is optimal for $M\ge0$; it ties the oracle on instance $0$, of mass
$3/4$, and its mean regret ratio is $M+7/4>M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reverse Markov bound]\label{thm:revmarkov}
If $X\le K$ pointwise and $t<K$ then
$\Prob[X>t]\ge\bigl(\E[X]-t\bigr)/(K-t)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split $\E[X]$ over $\{X>t\}$ and its complement: the tail contributes at most
$K\Prob[X>t]$ and the bulk at most $t(1-\Prob[X>t])$, so
$\E[X]\le t+(K-t)\Prob[X>t]$.
\end{proof}

Applied to the measured cell of Section~\ref{sec:cell}, with mean $4117/1000$,
cap $K=1179/140$ and $t=1$, this forces
$\Prob[\text{best static member loses}]\ge 0.42$: exactly the complement of the
$58\%$ winner share of $\rho$. The fat tail is not an outlier artifact.

\begin{definition}
For $X,Y:\Omega\to\N$, $X$ is \emph{stochastically dominated} by $Y$ if
$\Prob[X>t]\le\Prob[Y>t]$ for all $t\in\N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Finite layer cake]\label{lem:layercake}
If $X\le B$ pointwise then $\E[X]=\sum_{t=0}^{B-1}\Prob[X>t]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Exchange the order of summation in
$\sum_{t<B}\sum_\omega w(\omega)\mathbf 1[t<X(\omega)]$; the inner index set is
$\{0,\dots,X(\omega)-1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dominance implies a mean inequality; the converse fails]
\label{thm:dom}
If $X,Y\le B$ and $X$ is stochastically dominated by $Y$ then $\E[X]\le\E[Y]$.
Conversely, with $w=(1/2,1/2)$, $X=(0,10)$ and $Y=(6,6)$ we have
$\E[X]=5<6=\E[Y]$ yet $\Prob[X>6]=1/2>0=\Prob[Y>6]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:layercake} termwise; the counterexample is a computation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Safe eliminations]\label{thm:safe}
(i) If $c(\omega,a)\le c(\omega,b)$ for every $\omega$, deleting $b$ leaves the
oracle unchanged instancewise, hence in expectation. (ii) If $V(o,a)\le V(o,b)$
for every fiber $o$, deleting $b$ changes neither the optimal dial value nor the
best static value.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Deleting an element of a finite set that is weakly beaten by a surviving element
does not change the infimum; apply this pointwise to $c(\omega,\cdot)$ in (i) and
fiberwise to $V(o,\cdot)$ (and, via Corollary~\ref{cor:const}, to the static
values) in (ii).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mean-based elimination is unsafe]\label{thm:unsafe}
With $w=(3/4,1/4)$ and $c=\begin{pmatrix}1&5\\5&1\end{pmatrix}$ we have
$\E[c(\cdot,0)]=2$, $\E[c(\cdot,1)]=4$, oracle cost $1$ everywhere, and the
oracle of the sub-portfolio $\{0\}$ costs $2$: deleting the mean-worse member
doubles the oracle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Share realisability]\label{thm:shares}
Let $P>1$ and consider the diagonal portfolio on $\Omega=S$ with
$c(\omega,s)=1$ if $s=\omega$ and $P$ otherwise. For every probability vector $p$
on $S$, sampling with weights $p$ gives oracle winner shares exactly $p$,
expected cost $p(s)+(1-p(s))P$ for member $s$, and static ranking equal to the
ranking of $p$.
\end{theorem}

Consequently a measured share vector is a statement about the instance
\emph{sampler}, not about the algorithms: any share vector is realisable.

\section{The hidden channel is number-theoretic}

\begin{definition}[Powersmoothness]
$n$ is \emph{$B$-powersmooth} if every prime power $p^k$ dividing $n$ satisfies
$p^k\le B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Strict invisibility of the channel]\label{thm:invisible}
There are balanced semiprimes with identical visible profile --- both exactly
$21$ bits, both prime factors exactly $11$ bits --- of opposite $256$-smoothness
class. Explicitly, $N_1=1051\cdot1033=1085683$ has $1051-1=2\cdot3\cdot5^2\cdot7$
and $1033-1=2^3\cdot3\cdot43$, both $256$-powersmooth, while
$N_2=1319\cdot1307=1723933$ has $1319-1=2\cdot659$ and $1307-1=2\cdot653$ with
$659,653>256$ prime, so neither is $256$-powersmooth.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All four factors lie in $[2^{10},2^{11})$ and both products lie in
$[2^{20},2^{21})$; the smoothness claims are the displayed factorisations.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A capped probe detects the smooth class]\label{thm:probeworks}
Let $p$ be prime with $p-1$ $B$-powersmooth and put $L=\operatorname{lcm}(1,\dots,B)$.
Then $p\mid a^{L}-1$ for every integer $a$ with $p\nmid a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $p-1=\prod_i q_i^{k_i}$; each $q_i^{k_i}\le B$ divides $L$, so $p-1\mid L$.
By Fermat's little theorem $a^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p$, hence $a^{L}\equiv1\pmod p$.
\end{proof}

Thus $\gcd(a^{L}\bmod N-1,\,N)$ exposes such a $p$, and a budget-capped $p-1$
computation is a genuine one-sided observation of the hidden coordinate; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:probe} it is worth buying exactly when its price is below the
static regret.

\section{Ordered probes and threshold schedules}

\begin{definition}[Decreasing differences]
For preordered $O$ and $S$, a map $F:O\times S\to\Q$ has \emph{decreasing
differences} if $F(o',s')-F(o',s)\le F(o,s')-F(o,s)$ whenever $o\le o'$ and
$s\le s'$.
\end{definition}

For a finite linear order $S$ and $f:S\to\Q$ let $\lam(f)$ be the least
minimiser of $f$.

\begin{theorem}[Discrete Topkis]\label{thm:topkis}
If $F$ has decreasing differences then $o\mapsto\lam(F(o,\cdot))$ is monotone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $o\le o'$ but $t:=\lam(F(o',\cdot))<s:=\lam(F(o,\cdot))$. Least-ness of
$s$ at $o$ forces $F(o,s)<F(o,t)$. Decreasing differences with $t\le s$ and
$o\le o'$ gives $F(o',s)-F(o',t)\le F(o,s)-F(o,t)<0$, i.e.\ $F(o',s)<F(o',t)$,
contradicting optimality of $t$ at $o'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Threshold optimality]\label{thm:threshold}
Let $O$ and $S$ be finite linear orders. If the fiber-value table $V$ has
decreasing differences in (observation, member), then
$\pi^\star(o)=\lam(V(o,\cdot))$ is monotone and attains $D(\varphi)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\pi^\star$ attains the fiberwise minima, hence $D(\varphi)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:dialopt}; monotonicity is Theorem~\ref{thm:topkis}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For a monotone rule the set of observations on which a given member is played is
an interval; for two members it is upward-closed, i.e.\ a literal threshold rule.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity of single crossing]\label{thm:necessity}
There is a $2\times2$ cost table without decreasing differences whose
fiberwise-optimal schedule is not monotone. Ordered observations alone do not
make optimal schedules ordered.
\end{theorem}

\section{The measured cell as an exact rational model}\label{sec:cell}

\begin{construction}
Let $\Omega=\{0,1,2,3,4\}\times\{0,1\}$. The first coordinate is the hidden
powersmoothness class --- equivalently, which member wins --- with masses
\[
\lambda=\Bigl(\tfrac{58}{100},\tfrac{345}{1000},\tfrac{45}{1000},
\tfrac{28}{1000},\tfrac1{500}\Bigr),
\]
matching the measured winner shares. The second coordinate is a visible bit, a
fair coin drawn independently, so $w(c_0,b)=\lambda(c_0)/2$, and the scheduler's
observation is $\varphi(c_0,b)=b$. Each member costs $1$ on the class it owns and
the penalty $P=1179/140\approx8.4214$ elsewhere.
\end{construction}

\begin{theorem}[Properties of the measured model]\label{thm:cell}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The oracle winner shares are exactly $(0.580,0.345,0.045,0.028,0.002)$.
\item Every member wins on a set of positive mass and loses on a set of positive
mass: no universal winner.
\item $\varphi$ is invisible with profile $m(s)=\lambda(s)+(1-\lambda(s))P$, both
fibers having mass $1/2$.
\item $B=m(0)=4117/1000=4.117$, $\E[\Or]=1$, and the static regret is exactly
$R=3117/1000=3.117$.
\item Every rule reading the visible bit costs at least $4.117$: the dial gain is
$0$.
\item The two-armed learned rule playing $\rho$ on one value of the bit and
$(p-1)@256$ on the other costs exactly $279385/56000\approx4.989$.
\item Buying the hidden class at price $\kappa$ beats the best static schedule
iff $\kappa<3.117$.
\item At least $42\%$ of the mass lies where the best static member loses.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) Member $s$ is uniquely cheapest exactly on the classes $c_0=s$, of mass
$\lambda(s)$, since $P>1$. (2) Follows, as $\lambda(s)\in(0,1)$. (3) The fiber
over $b$ is a copy of the class axis with weights $\lambda/2$, so
$V(b,s)=\tfrac12\bigl(\lambda(s)+(1-\lambda(s))P\bigr)=\mu(b)m(s)$. (4) By
Lemma~\ref{lem:invmean}, $B=\min_sm(s)$, and $m(s)=P-\lambda(s)(P-1)$ is
decreasing in $\lambda(s)$, so $B=m(0)=0.58+0.42\cdot\tfrac{1179}{140}=4.117$;
the oracle costs $1$ everywhere. (5) Theorem~\ref{thm:nodial}. (6) By
Theorem~\ref{thm:fiber} the cost is $\tfrac12(m(0)+m(1))$ with
$m(1)=164109/28000\approx5.8610$. (7) Theorem~\ref{thm:probe}. (8)
Theorem~\ref{thm:revmarkov} with $\E[X]=4.117$, $K=1179/140$, $t=1$.
\end{proof}

Every headline number is thus a theorem about an explicit rational portfolio. The
penalty $P=1179/140$ is calibrated so that the reverse Markov bound at $t=1$
returns exactly the complementary share $0.42$.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What the null result means.} The common reading of $\Delta=0.000$ is
``the tuner failed''. Theorems~\ref{thm:nodial} and \ref{thm:strictworse} say
otherwise: when the observation is invisible for the portfolio the do-nothing
dial is optimal, and any deviation on positive mass is strictly harmful. The
measured degradation of the learned rule is a lower-bound phenomenon, not a
fitting artifact.

\paragraph{What to compute instead of tuning.} Theorem~\ref{thm:dialopt} gives an
$O(\lvert\Omega\rvert\lvert S\rvert+\lvert O\rvert\lvert S\rvert)$ procedure
returning both the ceiling of every possible router on the given features and an
optimal router attaining it. Theorem~\ref{thm:stability} converts a measurement
of near-invisibility into an a-priori cap $2\varepsilon$ on that ceiling.
Theorem~\ref{thm:probe} reduces ``should we buy information?'' to comparing a
price with the static regret. None of these require training.

\paragraph{Evidence discipline.} Three asymmetries recur: near-invisibility caps
the gain but a null gain does not imply near-invisibility
(Theorems~\ref{thm:stability}, \ref{thm:noconverse}, \ref{thm:nullgain});
stochastic dominance implies a mean inequality but a mean comparison implies
nothing about elimination (Theorems~\ref{thm:dom}, \ref{thm:unsafe}); and a
portfolio-level gain says nothing about pairwise structure, even after
irredundance (Theorems~\ref{thm:hidden}, \ref{thm:irred}).

\paragraph{Scope and limitations.} Winner shares are a property of the sampler
(Theorem~\ref{thm:shares}), so all quantitative statements are conditional on the
instance distribution; a sampler over wide ranges dilutes channels --- Fermat's
method in particular --- relative to a balanced design. The theory is finite and
treats costs as deterministic given the instance; randomised members are handled
by replacing $c(\omega,s)$ with a conditional mean, which preserves every result
above (only linearity and nonnegativity of the weights are used). Capped runs are
modelled by censoring the cost at the cap, which is exactly the boundedness
hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:revmarkov}. Interleaved schedules are outside the
present model.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Paid smoothness probes as first-class observations.} Model a
budget-capped $p-1$ attempt as an observation with a known false-negative rate
and compute the dial value as a function of the cap, turning
Theorem~\ref{thm:probe} into an optimal-budget theorem.
\item \textbf{Noisy quantile channels.} If the observed quantile is the true
smoothness quantile perturbed by a stochastic kernel, when does single crossing
survive? Monotone likelihood ratio kernels are the natural candidate.
\item \textbf{Interleaved schedules.} Extend the ladder to time-sliced
portfolios, where the static benchmark becomes a simplex of time allocations.
\item \textbf{Sampler-invariant reporting.} Design a report separating algorithm
properties from sampler properties, e.g.\ dial values of a canonical family of
feature sets.
\item \textbf{Empirical $\varepsilon$ estimation.} Turn
Theorem~\ref{thm:stability} into a statistical procedure with confidence bands,
yielding a certified upper bound on the achievable gain of any router.
\item \textbf{Beyond pairwise measurement.} Is there a tractable subset-level
statistic that controls pairwise swap masses, given Theorem~\ref{thm:irred}?
\end{enumerate}

